.bd
PROGRAM
.in +5
.br
.bd
c1
- C code generator


.in -5
.bd
USAGE
.in +5
.br
.bd
c1 [options] ast.1 data.2 out.s


.in -5
.bd
FUNCTION
.in +5
.br
.bd
C1
is the third pass of the C compiler.  It reads the syntax tree
.bd
c0
wrote, walks it, and writes Z80 assembler.  The initialised data
.bd
c0
put in the second file is copied through to the output, so the ".s"
that comes out is the whole of the translation unit.

Code is generated by matching the shape of each subtree against a
table of rewrite rules and emitting what the matching rule says.  A
shape that no rule names is not silently dropped: the output carries a
marker where the code should have been, so that a missing production
fails the build rather than producing a program that quietly does
nothing.

The three file arguments are positional and all three are required.
The first two are what
.bd
c0
wrote; the third is the assembly to be written.

The assembly is correct but not tight.
.bd
Peep
is expected to run over it afterwards and is worth a few percent of
the text, which on a machine this size is the difference between a
program fitting and not.  The driver runs it for -O.

The options are:

.in +10
.ta 10
.ti -10
-v mask	Set the debugging verbosity to the hexadecimal
.bd
mask.
Present only in a compiler built with debugging; the masks are listed
if the pass is run with no arguments.

.in -10
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
ccc (1), c0 (1), peep (1), asz (1), astpp (1)


.in -5
.bd
DIAGNOSTICS
.in +5
.br
Errors are written to standard error and the exit status is non-zero.
A shape with no rule to match it is reported and marked in the output
rather than passed over.
